bdaf3c01dd070343778910b10384ea.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." 13194139535800 true] [17592186083885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535800 true] [17592186083885 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe heights (in meters) of the trees in a particular park are normally distributed with a mean of $9m$ and a standard deviation of $4m$. Assuming that the heights of the trees in the park are normally distributed, what is the probability that a randomly selected tree is less than $10m$ tall? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.1$ | $0.5557$ | $0.5596$ | $0.5636$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.5948$ | $0.5987$ | $0.6026$ |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6331$ | $0.6368$ | $0.6406$ |" 13194139535800 false] [17592186083885 1100 "The heights (in meters) of the trees in a particular park are normally distributed with a mean of $9\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{m}.$ Assuming that the heights of the trees in the park are normally distributed, what is the probability that a randomly selected tree is less than $10\\,\\text{m}$ tall? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |" 13194139535800 true] [17592186083885 1145 "Let $X$ represent the heights (in meters) of the trees in the park. Then we have $X∼N(9,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<10).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=P(X\\le 10)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{10-9}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.25) \\\\  & =Φ(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.25)=0.5987≈0.60.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of a randomly selected tree being shorter than $10\\,\\text{m}$ is approximately $0.60.$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366839 13194139535800 true] [17592186366838 1089 "q-132712" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535800 true] [17592186366838 1100 "In a factory, the amount of sugar on a cookie is normally distributed with a mean of $15$ grams and a standard deviation of $0.8$ grams. What is the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ | $\\,0.1492\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ |" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366838 1113 #uuid "73d7efe3-40f0-50bf-94e8-8fb8e2a025f2" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366838 1145 "Let $X$ represent the amount of sugar (in grams) on the cookies. Then we have $X∼N(15,{0.8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<14).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<14)=P(X\\le 14)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 14) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{14-15}{0.8}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.25)=0.1056≈0.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams is approximately $0.11.$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366840 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366841 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366842 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366843 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366844 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1041 "selection" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366844 13194139535800 true] [17592186366839 1110 #uuid "9fb79ea7-0075-5837-927e-2be2a361f053" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366840 1034 "0.17" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366840 1111 #uuid "f169b3dc-07e1-5561-b44c-145ccbc91899" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366841 1034 "0.09" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366841 1111 #uuid "d422fde6-5a1d-5dfb-9963-cdac5919e108" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366842 1034 "0.15" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366842 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366842 1111 #uuid "26175838-efe2-56b5-aadc-6f37324df82f" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366843 1034 "0.21" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366843 1111 #uuid "da8374ea-7146-5037-afb1-4fcbd9c1aec0" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366844 1034 "0.11" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366844 1111 #uuid "59f17cf5-492c-5be7-91c9-2060fd7a87f2" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366846 13194139535800 true] [17592186366845 1089 "q-132852" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535800 true] [17592186366845 1100 "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366845 1113 #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366845 1145 "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366847 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366848 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366849 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366850 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366851 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1041 "selection" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366847 13194139535800 true] [17592186366846 1110 #uuid "b88c9f33-1bd4-5226-9952-5607a85200c7" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366847 1034 "$2.69$ years" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366847 1111 #uuid "4cdc2d3e-95b2-5055-8968-927437d8da0e" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366848 1034 "$2.74$ years" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366848 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366848 1111 #uuid "f4ac9d11-eacf-56f7-93fd-ebde418cf51b" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366849 1034 "$2.65$ years" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366849 1111 #uuid "60c04aee-7859-5b3a-b732-65288ee8345a" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366850 1034 "$2.57$ years" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366850 1111 #uuid "c8ab57a3-32ef-5d3a-ac3f-674533b88ff5" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366851 1034 "$2.79$ years" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366851 1111 #uuid "489cc168-b1ab-5540-b902-b1e06d3ab1e4" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366852 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366853 13194139535800 true] [17592186366852 1089 "q-132856" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366852 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535800 true] [17592186366852 1100 "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.7.$ In addition, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.75\\mu $ and $$1.25\\mu .$ Find the average cinema admission fee, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366852 1113 #uuid "50c01113-7eba-59c9-b302-374966a56f93" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366852 1145 "Let $X$ represent an admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & =1.8808 \\\\ 0.25\\mu  & =1.31656 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.31656}{0.25} \\\\  & ≈5.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366854 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366855 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366856 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366857 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366858 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1041 "selection" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366854 13194139535800 true] [17592186366853 1110 #uuid "d28733a3-72ae-5e83-8386-01017c625f20" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366854 1034 "$$5.27$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366854 1111 #uuid "2c848ccc-179b-57e9-82d4-ba54ed3a5d81" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366855 1034 "$$3.73$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366855 1111 #uuid "986983e8-eff1-586a-9b17-e1a646ae66c6" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366856 1034 "$$4.85$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366856 1111 #uuid "169778b1-f30d-506c-9b79-388d478ba232" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366857 1034 "$$4.41$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366857 1111 #uuid "a2ede810-33f1-565d-81ce-b556572146bb" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366858 1034 "$$5.38$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535800 true] [17592186366858 1111 #uuid "81a10ef6-7e42-5825-9004-f402e04dd26f" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366859 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366860 13194139535800 true] [17592186366859 1089 "q-132860" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366859 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535800 true] [17592186366859 1100 "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.75.$ Additionally, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.7\\mu $ and $$1.3\\mu .$ Find the percentage of cases when the admission fee is lower than $$5.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366859 1113 #uuid "23fbfc78-d3e6-58a5-85fb-02e846b95199" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366859 1145 "Let $X$ represent a randomly selected admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.75}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<5).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97) \\\\ 0.4\\mu  & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\mu  & =1.8808 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.8808}{0.4} \\\\  & ≈4.702.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(Z<\\frac{5-4.702.}{0.75}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.6554 \\\\  & ≈65.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately in $65.5%$ of cases, the admission fee is lower than $$5.$" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366861 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366862 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366863 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366864 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366865 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1041 "selection" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366863 13194139535800 true] [17592186366860 1110 #uuid "44264643-2cfb-5e42-897c-377536cb8eab" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366861 1034 "66.3%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366861 1111 #uuid "a6b5ac2c-3640-55e5-9b37-6d859a73cb5d" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366862 1034 "73.2%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366862 1111 #uuid "b1b0b6eb-b2a0-526b-9417-9c21d1b399ec" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366863 1034 "65.5%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366863 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366863 1111 #uuid "78d457d5-ae68-59ca-8ab4-ee2f3ba32db8" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366864 1034 "72.6%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366864 1111 #uuid "66a69e35-cd9b-5f62-b76a-e8f71b548f50" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366865 1034 "69.5%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366865 1111 #uuid "f571f37a-f2a6-5948-a5af-f4a701a54f36" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366866 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366867 13194139535800 true] [17592186366866 1089 "q-133053" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366866 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535800 true] [17592186366866 1100 "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366866 1113 #uuid "45137841-ca0a-5be6-86ab-33c4b038822e" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366866 1145 "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter." 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366868 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366869 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366870 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366871 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366872 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1041 "selection" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366871 13194139535800 true] [17592186366867 1110 #uuid "ee088655-a3b2-59e4-a930-adc3c565f0b7" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366868 1034 "8.9%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366868 1111 #uuid "c39c204c-5f5d-5cdf-9fc7-3f62344cd7ba" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366869 1034 "11.5%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366869 1111 #uuid "2e42cddd-68a6-5a67-bf44-5d2b270707d7" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366870 1034 "9.6%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366870 1111 #uuid "109d914c-8671-5252-98f3-d900c3d60264" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366871 1034 "7.9%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366871 1111 #uuid "83940ba6-93cc-541f-8381-de1561768377" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366872 1034 "8.1%" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535800 true] [17592186366872 1111 #uuid "5f060a3b-332c-5c3a-922d-4256f29a604a" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366873 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366874 13194139535800 true] [17592186366873 1089 "q-139243" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535800 true] [17592186366873 1100 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366873 1113 #uuid "6a2d2205-fb3a-59f9-b52e-16a95bd6d1ed" 13194139535800 true] [17592186366873 1145 "If a large data set is n